Direct regression on 100 random 16-type batches from the 80-relation pool shows BCS explains 22\% of cross-batch accuracy variance ($R^2 = 0.218$, $p < 0.001$). DocRED pooled $R^2 = 0.578$ ($n = 110$; semipartial $R^2_{\text{BCS}} = 0.056$ after centrality; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_partial_corr}). The typical BCS span (IQR = 0.054) yields an expected accuracy gap of 2.2\,pp (95\% CI [0.4, 4.0]). Quartile comparison confirms: bottom-quartile BCS batches average 82.8\% vs.\ 78.9\% for top-quartile ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp). The amplification from pairwise $r^2 \approx 1.5\%$ to batch-level $R^2 \approx 22\%$ is not a statistical artifact but the operative mechanism. Correlated confusable-type interactions compound within shared prompts \citep{robinson-1950-ecological,baan-2023-ecological}, producing the batch-level signal that makes BCS practically predictive (${\sim}3\times$ amplification illustrated by Monte Carlo simulation; Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}).

\paragraph{Cross-model validation.}
\looseness=-1
The effect replicates on Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: extreme-BCS gaps of $-$27.1\,pp (count 8) and $-$23.9\,pp (count 16), reaching 77--87\% of Qwen3-32B's gap (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}). All three open-weight families (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-70B, LLaMA-8B) show consistent negative BCS slopes on FewRel RE. Gemini 2.5 Flash provides partial but fragile corroboration ($\Delta = -17.6$\,pp at count 8) with substantial leverage sensitivity (leave-one-out Pearson: $r = -0.80 \to -0.42$, $p = 0.22$; Spearman: $\rho = -0.40 \to -0.18$, $p = 0.64$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_cross_model_gemini}). LLaMA-8B and LLaMA-70B both show no NER BCS signal ($r{=}{-}0.13$, $p{=}0.36$ for 70B), confirming the task asymmetry across three models (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_ner_null}).

\paragraph{Prompt sensitivity.}
\looseness=-1
Under the v2 prompt, the extreme-batch BCS gap is $-$25.5\,pp ($r = -0.919$, $p < 0.001$; $n{=}25$), modestly attenuated from v1 but highly significant. NER shows no batch-level BCS--F1 correlation under either prompt ($r \approx 0$, $n = 450$; \S\ref{sec:multi_channel}). The effect holds across four structurally distinct prompt variants at count 32 (all $r \leq -0.47$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_variants}), ruling out prompt design as the source. Few-shot demonstrations widen the confusability gap (+4.3\,pp under 5-shot), though this finding is limited to a single dataset and count level (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}).

\subsection{Multi-Dimensional Degradation}
\label{sec:multi_channel}

\looseness=-1
Error taxonomy decomposition (Table~\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} and Figure~\ref{fig:error_taxonomy}) shows intra-batch substitution (IntraSub) scales monotonically with BCS across all three datasets: $\rho = 1.0$ ($p < 0.001$) for Few-NERD NER, $\rho = 0.954$ ($p < 0.001$) for FewRel sentence-level RE, and $\rho = 0.843$ ($p < 10^{-30}$, $n = 110$) for DocRED document-level RE. The slightly lower DocRED correlation reflects additional noise from document-level context and cross-sentence reasoning. In NER, missed rates are higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic; confusable types impair entity detection itself. In DocRED, IntraSub accounts for 72\% of errors in high-BCS batches vs.\ 15\% in low-BCS batches; ExtraSub is zero (forced-choice format). Extreme-BCS batches produce $9.9\text{--}27.5\times$ more confusion than low-BCS batches. Cross-model replication confirms the IntraSub--BCS correlation on FewRel RE: Gemini~2.5~Flash ($\rho = 0.821$, $p < 0.001$, $n = 60$) and LLaMA-3.1-8B ($\rho = 0.430$, $p = 0.001$, $n = 54$; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_error_taxonomy}).

\looseness=-1
